# Line endings: LF in the repository and LF in the working tree, on every
# platform.
#
# This is not tidiness. internal/seed embeds fifteen JSON data files and
# seed.DataDigest() hashes their BYTES — deliberately, because hashing parsed
# values would need a canonical form of every book — and internal/room compares
# that digest at a room's gate. So a checkout that rewrote LF to CRLF announces
# a different digest from every other machine and is refused with
# `data_mismatch`.
#
# It really happened, and the numbers are worth keeping: a Mac hosting, a
# Windows machine joining, git's own `core.autocrlf=true` default on that
# machine, and no .gitattributes here to override it. Measured over the shipped
# fifteen:
#
#     as LF    df3bed25a5c5      (what hexarena-host prints)
#     as CRLF  f0ea65c2abb0
#
# ⚠️ And the data was not different. All fifteen files parse to identical
# values under CRLF — JSON allows \r between tokens, and a `\n` inside a string
# is the two characters `\` and `n`, which the rewrite never touches. The two
# machines would have fought the same battle; the gate refused a difference with
# no consequence. The defect is that the repository let the bytes differ for a
# reason that has nothing to do with the data, so it is fixed here rather than
# in the digest, which was doing exactly its job.
#
# Why both halves of the rule:
#
#   - `text=auto` lets git decide from the content, so a binary file added later
#     is left alone rather than corrupted. Nothing tracked today is binary — 471
#     files, all Go, SVG, golden, JSON, markdown and the module files, none of
#     them holding a NUL byte and none of them holding a CR — so this half buys
#     nothing yet and costs nothing.
#   - `eol=lf` is what actually pins the WORKING TREE. `text` alone leaves the
#     checkout to `core.autocrlf` / `core.eol`, and `native` on Windows means
#     CRLF, so `text=auto` on its own fixes the repository and not the machine
#     that was refused.
#
# Measured on git 2.55.0, cloning with `core.autocrlf=true` set: without the
# line below a .json and a .golden arrive with CRLF; with it they arrive with
# LF; and a file holding NUL bytes is byte-identical either way.
#
# The seventeen *.golden files get no rule of their own. They are byte-exact
# fixtures and `eol=lf` is exactly what they need — and `git check-attr text eol
# -- <a .golden>` answers `text: auto` / `eol: lf` from the single line below,
# checked rather than assumed. A second rule saying the same thing would be a
# second place for the two to disagree, which is the mistake CLAUDE.md records
# under "one source for a recorded string".
#
# ⚠️ ADDING THIS FILE DOES NOT REPAIR A CHECKOUT THAT ALREADY HAS CRLF ON DISK.
# Measured: pulling the commit that adds it leaves an existing Windows working
# tree exactly as it was, so the person who reported the bug is still refused
# after updating. That machine needs a fresh clone, or — in the clone it
# already has, after committing or stashing anything unfinished, because the
# second command discards uncommitted work:
#
#     git rm --cached -r . && git reset --hard
#
# which re-checks-out every file through the rule below. `hexarena-tui`'s join
# screen prints this binary's own data digest, so the two people can read the
# two short strings to each other and see the repair land.
* text=auto eol=lf
